ines (1) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained in Step 3 gives a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines supplied by the induction step are sunny, because the vertical lines are non‑sunny.  \n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base cases \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are verified directly by the constructions of Sections 1 and 2 and by the explicit family  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1,\\\\\n&\\text{sunny line through }(3,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\\\\\n&\\text{sunny line through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhich gives \\(k=2\\) for \\(n=3\\).  \nHence the induction described in Section 3 is anchored, and the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nWe have only \\(n\\) lines in total; therefore the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\).  \nConsequently the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the complete list of admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
