ready covered by \\(V\\) and\n   \\(H\\).  \n   All other diagonals \\(x+y=c\\;(c=5,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are kept.  \n   Thus we have exactly one sunny line.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Consider the longest diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n   \\qquad |\\Delta|=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Every sunny line meets \\(\\Delta\\) in at most one point*,\n   because two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\), which is\n   forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of\n   \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the\n   \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  A non‑sunny line is either vertical,\n   horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   * A vertical line \\(x=a\\) meets \\(\\Delta\\) only at \\((a,n+1-a)\\); in\n     particular the only vertical line that meets \\(\\Delta\\) at more than\n     one point would have to be the whole diagonal, which is not vertical.\n   * A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) meets \\(\\Delta\\) only at \\((n+1-b,b)\\).\n   * A line of slope \\(-1\\) can coincide with at most one diagonal; thus\n     at most one of the non‑sunny lines can be the diagonal \\(\\Delta\\)\n     itself.\n\n   Consequently, among the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines at most **two**\n   (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) can meet\n   \\(\\Delta\\) at the two endpoints \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  All other\n   non‑sunny lines meet \\(\\Delta\\) in at most one interior point.\n\n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(\\Delta\\) that can be covered\n   by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{endpoints})\\;+\\;(n-k-2)\\;(\\text{interior points})\n   = n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   To cover the whole diagonal we need exactly \\(n-k\\) points of\n   \\(\\Delta\\) supplied by the non‑sunny lines.  Equality forces every\n   non‑sunny line (except possibly the two special one