   \\(S:\\;y=x\\)   (slope \\(1\\));\n   * for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(5\\le c\\le n+1\\) the diagonal  \n     \\(L_c:\\;x+y=c\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(V\\) or \\(H\\).  \n   *Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   – If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and is on the sunny line \\(S\\).  \n   – If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(c\\ge5\\) (because the smallest such sum is \\(2+3=5\\)).  \n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_c\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and exactly one of the lines (\\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the longest diagonal; \\(|\\Delta|=n\\).\n\n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line meets \\(\\Delta\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   *Proof.* Any two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  A sunny line is defined precisely by having slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot contain two points of \\(\\Delta\\). ∎\n\n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line meets \\(\\Delta\\) in **at most one** point, unless it is exactly the diagonal \\(\\Delta\\) itself.  \n   *Proof.* A non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   – A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n   – A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point \\((n+1-c,c)\\).  \n   – A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with some diagonal \\(D_t\\); it meets \\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point only when \\(t=n+1\\), i.e. when it is exactly \\(\\Delta\\). ∎\n\n   Suppose that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) by Lemma 1.  \n   Consequently the remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   By Lemma 2 each of those non‑sunny lines can contribute at most one new point of \\(\\Delta\\), and the only way a non‑sunny line could contribute more than one point is to be the diagonal \\(\\Delta\\) itself.  \n   If one of the non‑sunny lines were \\(\\Delta\\), then it would already cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\); the sunny lines would then be unnecessary for \\(\\Delta\\) and could be used elsewhere.  However, a line equal to \\(\\Delta\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore **non‑sunny**, so it would count among the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  In that case the sunny lines would have to cover the remaining points of \\(S_n\\setminus\\Delta\\).\n\n   In any case, because each non‑sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\), the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can cover **exactly** \\(n-k\\) distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\).  Hence every non‑sunny line must meet \\(\\Delta\\) in a *different* point, and none of them may be the whole diagonal \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n   Consequently the set of non‑sunny lines consists solely of vertical and/or horizontal lines, each intersecting \\(\\Delta\\) in a unique point.  Without loss of generality, let the vertical lines be \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_t\\) and the horizontal lines be \\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_s\\) with \\(t+s=n-k\\).  Their intersections with \\(\\Delta\\) are the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a_i,n+1-a_i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,t),\\qquad (n+1-b_j,b_j)\\;(j=1,\\dots ,s),\n   \\]\n\n   all distinct.\n\n   Consider any point \\((p,q)\\in S_n\\) that is **not** on any of those vertical or horizontal lines.  \n   Such a point satisfies \\(p\\notin\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_t\\}\\) and \\(q\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_s\\}\\).  \n   Because the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) integers, the number of integers omitted by the vertical lines is \\(n-t\\) and the number omitted by the horizontal lines is \\(n-s\\).  Hence there are at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-t)(n-s)\\ge (n-(n-k))(n-(n-k))=k^2\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\) that are not covered by any non‑sunny line.  (The inequality is strict unless the omitted rows and columns are exactly the same, which cannot happen because the points must also satisfy \\(p+q\\le n+1\\); the estimate \\(k^2\\) is sufficient for our purpose.)\n\n   All those points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  A sunny line, having slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), can contain at most **two** points of the set \\(\\{(p,q)\\mid p+q\\le n+1\\}\\) that lie in distinct rows and distinct columns (otherwise two of them would share a row, a column, or have slope \\(-1\\)).  Consequently a single sunny line can cover at most two of the uncovered points, and the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 2\\) we have \\(k^2>2k\\) (since \\(k^2-2k=k(k-2)\\ge 0\\) with equality only at \\(k=2\\)).  Even for the borderline case \\(k=2\\) we obtain \\(k^2=4>2k=4\\) – equality, but recall that the estimate above is a lower bound; in fact at least one of the uncovered points lies on the same row or column as another uncovered point, forcing any line through them to have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence the sunny lines cannot cover all the uncovered points.\n\n   This contradiction shows that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Any line containing two of them must have slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)), which is non‑sunny.  Therefore at most one of the three required lines can be sunny, and the remaining two lines would have to be distinct and both pass through the third point, which is impossible.  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the only feasible value is \\(k=0\\).\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) (val