"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and fully rigorous solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ for every }n\\ge 3,\\qquad \nk=1\\text{ for every }n\\ge 4\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*  \n   For \\(c\\ge 2\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b=c\\},\n   \\qquad \n   S_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,n+1}D_c .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(S_n\\) is exactly the set of points required in the statement; \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   All have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are non\u2011sunny) and are distinct.  \n   *Coverage:* for any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique\n   \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=1\\) (valid for \\(n\\ge 4\\)).**  \n   Take the following \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &V:\\;x=1, &&\\text{(vertical, non\u2011sunny)}\\\\\n   &H:\\;y=1, &&\\text{(horizontal, non\u2011sunny)}\\\\\n   &S:\\;y=x, &&\\text{(slope }1\\text{, the unique sunny line)}\\\\\n   &L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=5,6,\\dots ,n+1), &&\\text{(non\u2011sunny)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage:* let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).\n   * Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  \n     \u2013 If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(S\\).  \n     \u2013 If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge 2+3=5\\) (because the smallest\n       distinct pair \\(\\ge2\\) is \\((2,3)\\)).  Since also \\(c\\le n+1\\),\n       the line \\(L_c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines,\n   and exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\) (all \\(n\\ge 3\\)).**  \n\n   *Diagonal \\(\\Delta\\).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   the longest diagonal; \\(|\\Delta|=n\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.** A sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(\\Delta\\).  \n   *Proof.* Any two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  A sunny\n   line has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot pass through two\n   points of \\(\\Delta\\). \u220e\n\n   **Lemma 2.** A non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\),\n   unless it is exactly the line \\(\\Delta\\) itself.  \n   *Proof.* A non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal\n   (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   \u2013 A vertical line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \n   \u2013 A horizontal line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point \\((n+1-c,c)\\).  \n   \u2013 A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with some diagonal \\(D_t\\); it meets\n   \\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point only when \\(t=n+1\\), i.e. when it is\n   exactly \\(\\Delta\\). \u220e\n\n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.\n   By Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\); the\n   remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the \\(n-k\\)\n   non\u2011sunny lines.  Lemma\u202f2 shows that each non\u2011sunny line can contribute\n   at most one new point of \\(\\Delta\\), and the only way to contribute more\n   would be to be the whole diagonal \\(\\Delta\\), which is impossible because\n   \\(\\Delta\\) is already needed to cover the \\(n-k\\) distinct points.\n   Consequently **every** non\u2011sunny line is either vertical or horizontal,\n   and each of them meets \\(\\Delta\\) in a distinct point.\n\n   Let the numbers of vertical and horizontal non\u2011sunny lines be\n   \\(v\\) and \\(h\\) respectively; thus \\(v+h=n-k\\).\n   The vertical lines are \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_v\\) with\n   \\(a_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) distinct, and the horizontal lines are\n   \\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_h\\) with distinct \\(b_j\\).\n\n   **Uncovered points.**  \n   A point \\((p,q)\\in S_n\\) is *not* on any non\u2011sunny line precisely when\n   \\(p\\notin\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_v\\}\\) and \\(q\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_h\\}\\).\n   Hence the number of uncovered points equals  \n\n   \\[\n   U=(n-v)(n-h).\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(v+h=n-k\\) we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   U=(n-v)(n-h)=\\bigl(k+h\\bigr)\\bigl(k+v\\bigr)\n               =k^{2}+k(v+h)+vh\n               \\ge k^{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at least \\(k^{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by any\n   non\u2011sunny line.\n\n   **Capacity of sunny lines.**  \n   Take two distinct uncovered points \\((p_1,q_1)\\) and \\((p_2,q_2)\\).\n   Because their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different and their \\(y\\)-coordinates\n   are different, the slope of the line through them is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{q_2-q_1}{p_2-p_1}\\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   only when either \\(q_1=q_2\\) (horizontal), \\(p_1=p_2\\) (vertical) or\n   \\(q_2-q_1=-(p_2-p_1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  None of these can happen because\n   the points avoid all rows \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) and columns \\(\\{b_j\\}\\); therefore\n   the slope is **different** from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Consequently a sunny\n   line can contain **at most two** uncovered points (any three would force\n   two of them to share a row, a column, or have slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) uncovered points.\n\n   Combining with (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}\\le U\\le 2k .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 3\\) we have \\(k^{2}>2k\\), a contradiction.  \n   For \\(k=2\\) the inequality gives \\(U\\ge4\\) while the two sunny lines can\n   cover at most \\(4\\) points; equality would require that each sunny line\n   contain exactly two uncovered points.  But any two uncovered points lie\n   in distinct rows and columns, so the line through them has slope\n   \\(\\frac{q_2-q_1}{p_2-p_1}\\neq\\pm1,0,\\infty\\); thus it *is* sunny.\n   However, the four uncovered points form a \\(2\\times2\\) grid, and a\n   single sunny line cannot pass through two opposite corners of such a\n   grid without also passing through the other two corners, which would\n   give slope \\(-1\\).  Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover all four\n   points, again a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists** for any\n   \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Here  \n\n   \\[\n   S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line containing two of these points has slope \\(-1\\) (the line\n   \\(x+y=3\\)), which is non\u2011sunny.  Thus at most one of the three required\n   lines can be sunny, and the remaining two lines would have to be\n   distinct and both pass through the third point, which is impossible.\n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the only admissible value is \\(k=0\\).\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)) and for \\(k=1\\)\n(\\(n\\ge4\\)) with the impossibility proofs for \\(k\\ge2\\) (all \\(n\\)) and\nfor \\(k=1\\) when \\(n=3\\), we obtain the complete answer stated above. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,n+1}\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b=c\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of all points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\)\nlines.  A line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique\n\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  The lines are\npairwise distinct because their constant terms differ.  Hence a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &: x=1,\\\\\nH &: y=1,\\\\\nS &: y=x,\\\\\nL_c &: x+y=c\\qquad(c=5,6,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are vertical and horizontal (non\u2011sunny); \\(S\\) has slope\n\\(1\\) and is the unique sunny line; each \\(L_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is\nnon\u2011sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have different slopes\nor different intercepts.\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(S\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge2+3=5\\) (the smallest possible sum\n    with distinct \\(a,b\\ge2\\)).  Since also \\(c\\le n+1\\), the line\n    \\(L_c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines,\nand exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny.  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=1\\) sunny lines exists for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k\\ge2\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nDefine the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\Delta\\) contains \\(n\\) points, all belonging to \\(S_n\\).\n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n*Proof.* Any two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  A sunny\nline has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot contain two\npoints of \\(\\Delta\\). \u220e\n\n**Lemma 2.** A non\u2011sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\),\nunless it coincides with \\(\\Delta\\) itself.\n\n*Proof.* A non\u2011sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)),\nor has slope \\(-1\\).  A vertical line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a\nline of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with some diagonal \\(D_t\\) and meets\n\\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point only when \\(t=n+1\\), i.e. when it is\nexactly \\(\\Delta\\). \u220e\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\);\nthe remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  Lemma\u202f2 forces each of those non\u2011sunny lines\nto meet \\(\\Delta\\) in exactly one point, and none of them can be the\nwhole diagonal \\(\\Delta\\).  Consequently every non\u2011sunny line is either\nvertical or horizontal, and the set of non\u2011sunny lines consists of\n\\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_v\\) and \\(h\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_h\\) with \\(v+h=n-k\\).  Their intersections with\n\\(\\Delta\\) are distinct, so the numbers \\(a_i,b_j\\) are all distinct.\n\nA point \\((p,q)\\in S_n\\) is not on any non\u2011sunny line precisely when\n\\(p\\notin\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_v\\}\\) and \\(q\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_h\\}\\).\nHence the number of *uncovered* points equals  \n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(v+h=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h)=\\bigl(k+h\\bigr)\\bigl(k+v\\bigr)\n            =k^{2}+k(v+h)+vh\\ge k^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus at least \\(k^{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by any\nnon\u2011sunny line.\n\nConsider any two uncovered points \\((p_1,q_1)\\) and \\((p_2,q_2)\\).\nBecause their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different and their \\(y\\)-coordinates\nare different, the slope of the line through