            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,n+1}\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b=c\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of all points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\)\nlines.  A line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique\n\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_c\\).  The lines are\npairwise distinct because their constant terms differ.  Hence a\nconfiguration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &: x=1,\\\\\nH &: y=1,\\\\\nS &: y=x,\\\\\nL_c &: x+y=c\\qquad(c=5,6,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are vertical and horizontal (non‑sunny); \\(S\\) has slope\n\\(1\\) and is the unique sunny line; each \\(L_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is\nnon‑sunny.  The lines are distinct because they have different slopes\nor different intercepts.\n\n*Coverage.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n  – If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(S\\).  \n  – If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge2+3=5\\) (the smallest possible sum\n    with distinct \\(a,b\\ge2\\)).  Since also \\(c\\le n+1\\), the line\n    \\(L_c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines,\nand exactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny.  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=1\\) sunny lines exists for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k\\ge2\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\))\n\nDefine the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\Delta\\) contains \\(n\\) points, all belonging to \\(S_n\\).\n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n*Proof.* Any two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\).  A sunny\nline has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot contain two\npoints of \\(\\Delta\\). ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.** A non‑sunny line contains at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\),\nunless it coincides with \\(\\Delta\\) itself.\n\n*Proof.* A non‑sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)),\nor has slope \\(-1\\).  A vertical line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a\nline of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with some diagonal \\(D_t\\) and meets\n\\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point only when \\(t=n+1\\), i.e. when it is\nexactly \\(\\Delta\\). ∎\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\);\nthe remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Lemma 2 forces each of those non‑sunny lines\nto meet \\(\\Delta\\) in exactly one point, and none of them can be the\nwhole diagonal \\(\\Delta\\).  Consequently every non‑sunny line is either\nvertical or horizontal, and the set of non‑sunny lines consists of\n\\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_v\\) and \\(h\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_h\\) with \\(v+h=n-k\\).  Their intersections with\n\\(\\Delta\\) are distinct, so the numbers \\(a_i,b_j\\) are all distinct.\n\nA point \\((p,q)\\in S_n\\) is not on any non‑sunny line precisely when\n\\(p\\notin\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_v\\}\\) and \\(q\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_h\\}\\).\nHence the number of *uncovered* points equals  \n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(v+h=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h)=\\bigl(k+h\\bigr)\\bigl(k+v\\bigr)\n            =k^{2}+k(v+h)+vh\\ge k^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus at least \\(k^{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by any\nnon‑sunny line.\n\nConsider any two uncovered points \\((p_1,q_1)\\) and \\((p_2,q_2)\\).\nBecause their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different and their \\(y\\)-coordinates\nare different, the slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{q_2-q_1}{p_2-p_1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\notherwise the points would share a row, a column, or lie on a line of\nslope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of uncovered.  Therefore a\nsunny line can contain **at most two** uncovered points; with \\(k\\) sunny\nlines we can cover at most \\(2k\\) uncovered points.\n\nCombining with (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nk^{2}\\le U\\le 2k .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k\\ge3\\) we have \\(k^{2}>2k\\), an impossibility.  For \\(k=2\\) the\ninequality forces \\(U=4\\) and each sunny line must contain exactly two\nuncovered points.  The four uncovered points form a \\(2\\times2\\) grid;\nany line through two opposite corners of this grid also passes through the\nother two corners, giving slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny\nline.  Hence the two sunny lines cannot cover all four points, a\ncontradiction.\n\nThus **no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists** for any\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line containing two of these points has slope \\(-1\\) (the line\n\\(x+y=3\\)), which is non‑sunny.  Consequently at most one of the three\nrequired lines can be sunny, and the remaining two distinct lines would\nboth have to pass through the third point, which is impossible.  Hence\nfor \\(n=3\\) the only admissible value is \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\n- For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines\n  exists (the family of lines \\(x+y=c\\)).\n- For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n  exists (the four‑type construction above).\n- No configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\),\n  and for \\(n=3\\) the case \\(k=1\\) is also impossible.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ for all }n\\ge3,\\qquad\n       k=1\\text{ for all }n\\ge4\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
