elta\\) itself.\n\n*Proof.* A non‑sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)),\nor has slope \\(-1\\).  A vertical line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at the unique point\n\\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal line meets \\(\\Delta\\) at \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a\nline of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with some diagonal \\(D_t\\) and meets\n\\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point only when \\(t=n+1\\), i.e. when it is\nexactly \\(\\Delta\\). ∎\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\);\nthe remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(\\Delta\\) must be covered by the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Lemma 2 forces each of those non‑sunny lines\nto meet \\(\\Delta\\) in exactly one point, and none of them can be the\nwhole diagonal \\(\\Delta\\).  Consequently every non‑sunny line is either\nvertical or horizontal, and the set of non‑sunny lines consists of\n\\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_v\\) and \\(h\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_h\\) with \\(v+h=n-k\\).  Their intersections with\n\\(\\Delta\\) are distinct, so the numbers \\(a_i,b_j\\) are all distinct.\n\nA point \\((p,q)\\in S_n\\) is not on any non‑sunny line precisely when\n\\(p\\notin\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_v\\}\\) and \\(q\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_h\\}\\).\nHence the number of *uncovered* points equals  \n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h).\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(v+h=n-k\\),\n\n\\[\nU=(n-v)(n-h)=\\bigl(k+h\\bigr)\\bigl(k+v\\bigr)\n            =k^{2}+k(v+h)+vh\\ge k^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus at least \\(k^{2}\\) points of \\(S_n\\) are not covered by any\nnon‑sunny line.\n\nConsider any two uncovered points \\((p_1,q_1)\\) and \\((p_2,q_2)\\).\nBecause their \\(x\\)-coordinates are different and their \\(y\\)-coordinates\nare different, the slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{q_2-q_1}{p_2-p_1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\notherwise the points would share a row, a column, or lie on a line of\nslope \\(-1\\), contradicting the