            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=1 } .\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\;\\Big|\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set of points that have to be covered).  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) put  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\n   \\]\n\n   the “diagonal’’ of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   In particular  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta:=D_{\\,n+1}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is the longest diagonal; \\(|\\Delta|=n\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(L_c:\\;x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) are all non‑sunny\n   (slope \\(-1\\)) and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) consider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &V:\\;x=1, &&\\text{vertical (non‑sunny)}\\\\\n   &H:\\;y=1, &&\\text{horizontal (non‑sunny)}\\\\\n   &S:\\;y=x, &&\\text{the unique sunny line}\\\\\n   &L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=5,6,\\dots ,n+1), &&\\text{slope }-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).\n   * Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  \n     – If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(S\\).  \n     – If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge 5\\) (the smallest sum with\n       \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\) is \\(2+3\\)).  Since also \\(c\\le n+1\\),\n       the line \\(L_c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line (\\(S\\))\n   is sunny.  Consequently \\(k=1\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   A sunny line meets the diagonal \\(\\Delta\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n   *Proof.*  Any two distinct points of \\(\\Delta\\) have slope \\(-1\\); a sunny\n   line has a different slope, hence cannot contain two of them. ∎\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   A non‑sunny line meets \\(\\Delta\\) in at most one point, and it meets\n   \\(\\Delta\\) in more than one point **iff** it is exactly the line\n   \\(\\Delta\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=n+1\\)).\n\n   *Proof.*  A non‑sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal\n   (\\(y=c\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\).  The first two intersect \\(\\Delta\\) in the\n   unique point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) or \\((n+1-c,c)\\).  A line of slope \\(-1\\) is\n   some diagonal \\(D_t\\); it coincides with \\(\\Delta\\) only when \\(t=n+1\\). ∎\n\n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly\n   \\(s\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  Let the remaining \\(n-s\\) lines be\n   non‑sunny.\n\n   *Step 1 – the diagonal must be covered pointwise.*  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most one point of \\(\\Delta\\);\n   by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most one point of\n   \\(\\Delta\\) unless it is \\(\\Delta\\) itself.  If one of the non‑sunny\n   lines were \\(\\Delta\\), then all points of \\(\\Delta\\) would already be\n   covered and we would have \\(s=0\\), contradicting \\(s\\ge2\\).  Hence **no**\n   line equals \\(\\Delta\\), and consequently every line (sunny or not)\n   meets \\(\\Delta\\) in exactly one point.  Because \\(|\\Delta|=n\\), the\n   \\(n\\) lines must meet \\(\\Delta\\) in *pairwise distinct* points; thus\n   each line is uniquely identified by the point of \\(\\Delta\\) it contains.\n\n   *Step 2 – description of the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   A non‑sunny line that is not \\(\\Delta\\) is either vertical, horizontal,\n   or a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\neq n+1\\).  Such a diagonal does **not**\n   intersect \\(\\Delta\\) at all, contradicting Step 1.  Therefore every\n   non‑sunny line is vertical or horizontal.  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   V=\\{x=a_1,\\dots ,x=a_v\\},\\qquad\n   H=\\{y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_h\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(v+h=n-s\\).  The points of \\(\\Delta\\) lying on these lines are\n   \\((a_i,n+1-a_i)\\) and \\((n+1-b_j,b_j)\\); they are all distinct, so the\n   sets \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{b_j\\}\\) are disjoint.\n\n   *Step 3 – points not covered by the vertical/horizontal lines.*  \n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is uncovered by all non‑sunny lines precisely\n   when \\(a\\notin\\{a_i\\}\\) and \\(b\\notin\\{b_j\\}\\).  Hence the number of\n   uncovered points equals  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\sum_{\\substack{a\\notin\\{a_i\\}\\\\ b\\notin\\{b_j\\}\\\\ a+b\\le n+1}}1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because the sets \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{b_j\\}\\) are disjoint, the\n   smallest possible values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that are not excluded are\n   \\(a_{\\min}=v+1\\) and \\(b_{\\min}=h+1\\).  Consequently every pair\n   \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge v+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1\n   \\]\n\n   belongs to the uncovered set.  The number of such pairs is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   U\\ge (n-v-h)\\,(n-v-h+1)/2 .\n   \\t