\\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n     – If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(L_3\\).  \n     – If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge5\\) (the smallest possible sum with\n       \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\) is \\(2+3\\)).  Since also \\(c\\le n+1\\),\n       the line \\(L_c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines,\n   and exactly one line (\\(L_3\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is possible for all\n   \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that no configuration with at least two\n   sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   Any non‑sunny line is vertical (\\(x=1\\)), horizontal (\\(y=1\\)) or has slope\n   \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=4\\), which contains none of the three points).  \n   If at most one of the three lines is non‑sunny, at least two lines are\n   sunny.  Two sunny lines are either parallel to each other or intersect in a\n   point that has either the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate or the same \\(y\\)‑coordinate\n   as one of the three points, forcing that point to lie on a non‑sunny line.\n   Consequently the three points cannot be covered by only one non‑sunny line,\n   and a configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) does not exist.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).  Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a family\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing\n   \\(s\\ge2\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Let \\(D_n\\) be the diagonal defined above.  Any two distinct points of\n   \\(D_n\\) have slope \\(-1\\); therefore a line that contains two points of\n   \\(D_n\\) must have slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{(1)}\\qquad\\text{a line that is not of slope \\(-1\\) meets }D_n\\text{ in at most