e that no configuration with at least two sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Lemma 1 (Diagonal reduction).*  \n   If the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (i.e. the whole diagonal \\(D_n\\)) belongs to a\n   covering family, then deleting this line leaves a covering of\n   \\(T_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines is unchanged.\n\n   *Proof.*  Every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\); such a point is also in \\(T_n\\) and therefore is covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines. \u220e\n\n   *Lemma 2 (No sunny line without the maximal diagonal).*  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and does **not**\n   contain the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Then **all** non\u2011sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are\n   vertical or horizontal, and each of them meets the diagonal \\(D_n\\) in exactly one\n   point. Consequently the set of vertical\u2011line abscissae \\(V\\) and the set of\n   horizontal\u2011line ordinates \\(H\\) satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   V\\cup\\{\\,n+1-y\\mid y\\in H\\,\\}= \\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   V\\cap\\{\\,n+1-y\\mid y\\in H\\,\\}= \\varnothing . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Any line whose slope is \\(-1\\) is parallel to \\(D_n\\) and therefore cannot contain any\n   point of \\(D_n\\); such a line would leave at least one point of \\(D_n\\) uncovered,\n   contradicting the hypothesis that the \\(n\\) lines cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\).\n   Hence every non\u2011sunny line must be vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)).\n   A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(D_n\\) at \\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal line\n   \\(y=c\\) meets \\(D_n\\) at \\((n+1-c,c)\\).  Because the \\(n\\) lines are distinct,\n   the \\(n\\) intersection points with \\(D_n\\) are distinct, which yields (1). \u220e\n\n   *Lemma 3 (Counting contradiction).*  \n   Under the assumptions of Lemma\u202f2, let  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\}\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\bigl(V\\cup\\{\\,n+1-y\\mid y\\in H\\,\\}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   By (1) we have \\(|S|=s\\).  For each \\(a\\in S\\) the point\n   \\((a,n+1-a)\\in D_n\\) is **not** covered by any vertical or horizontal line,\n   hence must be covered by a sunny line.  Two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) lie on a\n   line of slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one**\n   point of \\(D_n\\).  Consequently at least \\(s\\) sunny lines are needed to\n   cover the \\(s\\) points \\(\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid a\\in S\\}\\).\n\n   Moreover, for each \\(a\\in S\\) the point \\((a,n-a)\\) (which satisfies\n   \\(a+(n-a)=n\\)) must also be covered.  It cannot lie on a sunny line,\n   because all points with sum \\(n\\) belong to the line \\(x+y=n\\) whose slope is\n   \\(-1\\).  Hence \\((a,n-a)\\) must be covered by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a\n   horizontal line \\(y=n-a\\).  Since \\(a\\notin V\\) by definition of \\(S\\), we must\n   have \\(n-a\\in H\\).  Thus the map \\(a\\mapsto n-a\\) embeds \\(S\\) into \\(H\\), giving\n   \\(|H|\\ge s\\).  By symmetry we also obtain \\(|V|\\ge s\\).\n\n   Adding the two inequalities yields  \n\n   \\[\n   |V|+|H|\\ge 2s .\n   \\]\n\n   But the total number of non\u2011sunny lines equals \\(|V|+|H|=n-s\\) (there are \\(n\\) lines\n   altogether and exactly \\(s\\) of them are sunny).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   n-s\\ge 2s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n\\ge 3s .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(s\\ge2\\), we would need \\(n\\ge6\\).  Assume \\(n\\ge6\\) and pick any\n   \\(a\\in S\\).  The three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,1),\\;(a,2),\\;(a,3)\n   \\]\n\n   all belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(a+3\\le n+1\\).  None of them is covered by a\n   vertical line (as \\(a\\notin V\\)) nor by a horizontal line (their ordinates\n   \\(1,2,3\\) are not in \\(H\\), otherwise the corresponding vertical line would\n   already meet \\(D_n\\) at the same point).  Hence each of these three points\n   must be covered by a sunny line