2\\).**  \n   We prove that no configuration with at least two sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Lemma\u202f1 (Reduction by the diagonal line).*  \n   If the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (i.e. the whole diagonal \\(D_n\\)) belongs to a covering family, then deleting this line leaves a covering of \\(T_{n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) distinct lines, and the number of sunny lines is unchanged.\n\n   *Proof.*  Every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\); such a point is also in \\(T_n\\) and therefore is covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines. \u220e\n\n   Hence, to prove the impossibility for all \\(n\\) it suffices to treat the case in which the diagonal line is **absent**; the general case then follows by repeatedly applying Lemma\u202f1 (induction on \\(n\\)).\n\n   From now on assume that the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and does **not** contain the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   *Lemma\u202f2 (Structure of non\u2011sunny lines).*  \n   Under the above assumption every non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)). Moreover each such line meets the diagonal \\(D_n\\) in exactly one point, and the \\(n\\) intersection points are all distinct.\n\n   *Proof.*  A line whose slope is \\(-1\\) is parallel to \\(D_n\\) and therefore cannot meet any point of \\(D_n\\); if such a line were present, at least one point of \\(D_n\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(T_n\\) (in particular the \\(n\\) points of \\(D_n\\)) are covered.  A line with slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) meets \\(D_n\\) exactly once, at \\((c,n+1-c)\\) or \\((n+1-c,c)\\) respectively.  Since the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are distinct, the \\(n\\) intersection points are distinct. \u220e\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   V=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad \n   H=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n   \\qquad \n   S=\\{a\\mid (a,n+1-a)\\in D_n\\text{ is covered by a sunny line}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n 