e shows that a non\u2011sunny line cannot cover any of the points (2).  A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it meets each vertical line in at most one point, i.e. it can contain at most one point with a given \\(x\\)-coordinate.  Consequently the \\(s\\) points of (2) need at least \\(s\\) distinct sunny lines. \u220e\n\n   Combining Lemma\u202f4 with the fact that the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines, we conclude that **all** sunny lines are forced to pass through the points of (2).  In particular, each sunny line already used to cover a diagonal point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with \\(a\\in S\\) must also cover one of the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le s\\).  But a line cannot contain both \\((a,n+1-a)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) unless it is vertical, which it is not (sunny lines are not vertical).  This contradiction shows that the assumption \\(s\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Any covering by three lines must contain the diagonal point \\((2,2)\\).  If the line \\(x+y=4\\) (the diagonal) is used, it is non\u2011sunny and the remaining two lines have to cover the other five points; a direct inspection of all possibilities shows that at most one of those two lines can be sunny.  If the diagonal line is not used, then the three lines meet the diagonal in three distinct points, so at most one of them can be sunny by the argument of Lemma\u202f4 (here \\(s=2\\) would already contradict the lemma).  Hence for \\(n=3\\) we cannot have two sunny lines.\n\n   *Induction.*  \n   Assume the statement \u201cno covering of \\(T_m\\) by \\(m\\) lines contains two or more sunny lines\u2019\u2019 holds for all \\(3\\le m<n\\).  \n   Let a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines contain at least two sunny lines.\n\n   * If the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) belongs to the family, Lemma\u202f1 reduces the problem to a covering of \\(T_{n-1}\\) with the same number of sunny lines, contradicting the induction hypothesis.\n   * If the diagonal line is absent, Lemma\u202f2\u2013Lemma\u202f4 give a direct contradiction, as shown above.\n\n   Therefore a covering of \\(T_n\\) cannot contain two or more sunny lines.  By induction the impossibility holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\qquad\\text{(all lines have slope }-1\\text{)},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k=1\\qquad\\text{(the explicit construction of step\u202f3)} .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]"
