            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completed the proof.  \nThe only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*  \n   For a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   D_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the set of points that have to be covered and the “main diagonal’’ of the triangle).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   all have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are non‑sunny) and each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines works  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_1:\\;x=1 &&\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_2:\\;y=1 &&\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_3:\\;y=x &&\\text{(slope \\(1\\), sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=5,6,\\dots ,n+1) &&\\text{(slope \\(-1\\), non‑sunny)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_1\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_2\\).\n   * Otherwise \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  \n     – If \\(a=b\\) then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_3\\).  \n     – If \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\ge5\\) (the smallest possible sum with\n       \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\) is \\(2+3\\)).  Because also \\(c\\le n+1\\),\n       the line \\(x+y=c\\) belongs to the list and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one listed line and exactly one line (\\(\\ell_3\\)) is sunny.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is possible for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   The proof proceeds in three steps.\n\n   *Step 1 – No line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \n     The diagonal \\(D_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) points.  \n     Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, each point of \\(D_n\\) must be covered\n     by a different line.  \n     A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. of slope \\(-1\\)) is parallel to every\n     point of \\(D_n\\) and therefore cannot contain any of them.  Consequently\n     **no line in the family can have slope \\(-1\\)**.  Every line is therefore\n     either vertical, horizontal, or sunny.\n\n   *Step 2 – A partition of the columns.*  \n     For a vertical line \\(x=i\\) we say that it *occupies* column \\(i\\).  \n     A horizontal line \\(y=j\\) meets the diagonal at the point\n     \\((n+1-j,j)\\); we say that it occupies column \\(n+1-j\\).  \n     A sunny line also meets the diagonal at a unique point, say\n     \\((c,n+1-c)\\); we say that it occupies column \\(c\\).  \n     Because the \\(n\\) diagonal points are covered by distinct lines,\n     the sets  \n\n     \\[\n     V=\\{i\\mid x=i\\text{ is a line}\\},\\qquad\n     H=\\{n+1-j\\mid y=j\\text{ is a line}\\},\\qquad\n     S=\\{c\\mid\\text{a sunny line meets }D_n\\text{ at }(c,n+1-c)\\}\n     \\]\n\n     are pairwise disjoint and their union is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n     Hence  \n\n     \\[\n     |V|+|H|+|S|=n,\\qquad |S|=k .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – A column belonging to a sunny line forces many sunny lines.*  \n\n     Let \\(c_0\\) be the **smallest** element of \\(S\\) (the smallest column\n     occupied by a sunny line).  \n     Because column \\(c_0\\) is not occupied by a vertical line,\n     the points  \n\n     \\[\n     P=\\{(c_0,1),(c_0,2),\\dots ,(c_0,k)\\}\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     are not covered by any vertical line.\n\n     **Claim.** No horizontal line can have ordinate \\(\\le k\\).  \n     Indeed, suppose a horizontal line \\(y=t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) existed.\n     It meets the diagonal at \\((n+1-t,t)\\); the column of that diagonal\n     point is \\(n+1-t\\).  Since \\(t\\le k\\) we have \\(n+1-t\\ge n+1-k\\ge c_0\\)\n     (because \\(c_0\\le k\\) by definition of \\(c_0\\)).  \n     The column \\(n+1-t\\) is already occupied either by a vertical line,\n     by another horizontal line, or by a sunny line.  In any case the\n     diagonal point \\((n+1-t,t)\\) would be covered twice, contradicting the\n     fact that each diagonal point is covered by exactly one line.  Hence such\n     a horizontal line cannot exist.\n\n     Consequently the points in (2) are not covered by any vertical or\n     horizontal line; they must be covered by sunny lines.  \n     A sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal, therefore it can contain\n     **at most one** point of a given column.  Hence the \\(k\\) points of (2)\n     require at least \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n     But the family already contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and each of\n     those lines already passes through a distinct diagonal point\n     \\((c,n+1-c)\\) with \\(c\\in S\\).  A sunny line that passes through a\n     diagonal point with column \\(c\\) cannot also pass through a point of\n     column \\(c\\) (otherwise it would be vertical).  Therefore the sunny\n     lines needed to cover the points of (2) must be **different** from the